CVPR,cs_conf,perception3d,Ehm_Teaching_DINOv3_About_Partial_3D_Geometry_A_Self-Supervised_Geomet,5,overview|qualitative,0,0,"Qualitative Results of Left-Right Prediction: Ge- oLoRA shows better chirality prediction than DINOv3 (shapes from BECOS). 4.7. Applications Chirality Classiﬁcation Distinguishing the left and right of a mesh is an essential task for 3D shape analysis. We train the network from [58] with different features: DI- NOv3, GeoLoRA, and features obtained by our pipeline, but with the PointInfoNCE loss. For training, we use the BECOS full-to-full train set, and we evaluate on the partial shapes from the BECOS partial-to-full test set. From the results in Table 6, we observe that GeoLoRA provides sig-"
CVPR,cs_conf,perception3d,Ehm_Teaching_DINOv3_About_Partial_3D_Geometry_A_Self-Supervised_Geomet,6,overview|curves|failure,0,0,"Real-World Scan: We match a real-world partial scan on a template human. DINOv3 struggles to characterise incom- plete geometry, while GeoLoRA shows robust performance. cations, such as interpreting data from acquisition pipelines. In Figure 6, we report an illustrative result, obtained from a depth-sensor-based body scanner. During the acquisition, the user was partially misaligned with respect to the captur- ing volume. While DINOv3 fails to recover the semantics of such ruined geometry, our features show robustness. We found this result really exciting, as it can open up new tech- niques fo"
