Nature,nature,life_sciences,W4404814344,4,curves,0,0,"Differential behavioural kernels show substantial heterogeneity across and within subjects, even when all subjects perform the task well. Behavioural kernels are a behaviour-based measure of how much weight the pulses from different time-points within a trial have on a subject’s decision (Methods and Supplementary Fig. 1). For a given feature, the differential behavioural kernel, shown here, is the difference in the behavioural kernel when that feature is in its relevant versus irrelevant context. Time axes run from the start of the auditory pulse trains (t = 0) to their end (t = 1.3 s)"
